Reason:
These macros interpret bits meant for present PMD entries, but when the entry is a non-present softleaf (device-private) PMD, the bit semantics differ. On x86-64, `_PAGE_SWP_SOFT_DIRTY` is aliased to `_PAGE_RW`, so `pmd_write()` returns the softdirty state instead of the writable state. This causes a writable migration entry to be installed when it should be read-only, corrupting rmap state and triggering assertion failures (e.g., `VM_WARN_ON_FOLIO` on `entire_mapcount > 1` and `PageAnonExclusive`).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
} else {
    softleaf_t old_entry;
    pmdval = pmdp_huge_get_and_clear(vma->vm_mm, address, pvmw->pmd);
    old_entry = softleaf_from_pmd(pmdval);
    writable = softleaf_is_device_private_write(old_entry);
    softdirty = pmd_swp_soft_dirty(pmdval);
    uffd_wp = pmd_swp_uffd_wp(pmdval);
}
...
if (present && pmd_young(pmdval))
    entry = make_migration_entry_young(entry);
if (present && pmd_dirty(pmdval)) {
    folio_mark_dirty(folio);
    entry = make_migration_entry_dirty(entry);
}
```
Reason:
The fix separates the handling of present and non-present (softleaf) PMD entries. For softleaf entries, it uses the correct accessors (`softleaf_is_device_private_write`, `pmd_swp_soft_dirty`, `pmd_swp_uffd_wp`) to interpret the entry’s fields, avoiding misinterpretation of bits. It also guards the A/D bit updates (`pmd_young`, `pmd_dirty`) with a `present` check because these bits are only meaningful for present entries; otherwise, `pmd_dirty()` on a softleaf entry could read stale or incorrect values. This ensures the migration entry is created with the correct attributes and prevents rmap corruption.

